ht split into two ranges by just computing the correct resulting operation:\n\nFor delete vs delete:\n\nIf A and B don't overlap (posA+lenA <= posB or posB+lenB <= posA):\n  - Simple position adjustments (cases 1 & 2 above)\n\nIf A and B overlap:\n  - Compute A's effective deletion: remove positions that B already deleted\n  - This might result in 0, 1, or 2 contiguous ranges\n  - If 0 ranges: nothing to delete for A\n  - If 1 range: single delete operation\n  - If 2 ranges: I'll apply them as separate operations\n  \nActually I just realized: in practice for a collaborative text editor, most concurrent edits won't be overlapping deletes of large ranges. Overlapping deletions happen when two people are deleting the same text, which is relatively rare. And the most common case is insert vs insert or insert vs delete.\n\nFor simplicity and robustness, let me implement OT as follows:\n\nFor the transform function, I'll handle all cases correctly:\n- Insert vs insert: position adjustment with tiebreaker\n- Insert vs delete: position adjustment \n- Delete vs insert: position adjustment\n- Delete vs delete: compute surviving range(s)\n\nBut I'll represent the result as possibly multiple operations (for delete vs delete overlap that splits the range). And the server applies a list of operations per version.\n\nActually, you know what, let me simplify further. Many simple OT implementations just handle operations on a string as:\n- Characters are indexed by position\n- Operations are insert(pos, str) or delete(pos, len)\n- Transform is defined as above\n- When the server receives concurrent operations, it transforms them sequentially\n\nThe trick with overlapping deletions: if we compute the deletion as \"delete whatever A wanted to delete that B hasn't already deleted\", and the result might be 0, 1, or 2 ranges. I'll just handle it by emitting separate operations.\n\nOK, but for efficiency and simplicity, let me just implement it for the common case and handle the edge case by converting to a string-based representation. Here's my plan:\n\n1. Apply all transformations positionally (common OT model)\n2. For overlapping deletions, just merge them (extend the range to cover the union) - this is simple and the resulting text is still correct (just deletes a bit more than strictly necessary, but both users said \"delete this area\")\n\nActually no, merging would delete MORE than either user intended. User A wants to delete \"abc\" and user B wants to delete \"cde\" → merge deletes \"abcde\", but A only wanted \"abc\" gone and B only wanted \"cde\" gone.\n\nHmm, but would users ever notice? If user A deletes \"abc\" and user B deletes \"cde\", after both operations, \"c\" should be gone, \"d\" should be gone, \"e\" should be gone, \"a\" should be gone, \"b\" should be gone. That's \"abcde\" all deleted. Which is exactly what the merge gives. So actually merging IS correct in this case!\n\nWait, is it? Let me think. User A explicitly wants to delete \"abc\". User B explicitly wants to delete \"cde\". After both operations: \"abcde\" are all deleted. The merge result says exactly that. So the merge IS correct.\n\nBut what if A deletes \"ab\" and B deletes \"de\" (non-overlapping)? Then:\n- Merge gives delete(0,5) → deletes \"abcde\"\n- But A only wanted \"ab\" gone and B only wanted \"de\" gone\n- \"c\" should remain!\n\nSo merging is wrong for non-overlapping cases. The correct result for A deletes \"ab\" (positions 0-1) and B deletes \"de\" (positions 3-4) in \"abcde\" is:\n- After A then B': A deletes 0-1 → \"cde\" → B' needs to delete B's chars at original positions 3-4, now at 1-2 in \"cde\" → \"ce\"\n- After B then A': B deletes 3-4 → \"abc\" → A' needs to delete A's chars at original positions 0-1, still at 0-1 → \"c\"\n\nThe result is \"c\" (from A then B') or \"c\" (from B then A'). Yes they converge! And \"c\" is left, which is correct (middle character not deleted by either).\n\nOK so the issue is only when deletions are interlea