+{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"16846457-3567-44ef-aee2-68f4ccb7be36","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me implement the WebSocket handler. This is the most complex part. I need to:\n\n1. Track which clients are connected to which documents\n2. When a client connects, send them a sync message with current content and version\n3. When a client sends an operation, transform it against any intervening ops, apply it, and broadcast\n4. Handle connection close\n\nLet me think about the server-side state:\n\nFor each document, I maintain:\n- A queue of operations (for OT transformation)\n- A set of connected WebSocket clients\n\nThe flow when a client sends an operation:\n1. Receive op with version V (the client's last known version)\n2. Check if there's a gap: if server version > V, transform the op against intervening ops\n3. Apply the transformed operation to get new content\n4. Increment the version\n5. Store the operation and new content in the database\n6. Broadcast the operation and new version to all clients (including the sender for ack)\n\nWait, actually I should broadcast to all OTHER clients (not the sender), and send an ack to the sender. Or just broadcast to everyone including the sender and the sender ignores it.\n\nActually, standard approach:\n- Broadcast the operation to all connected clients except the sender\n- Send an ack to the sender with the new version\n\nBut wait, the clients also need to apply the same OT transformation. If client A sent op at version 5, but the server is at version 7 (because B and C sent ops), then A's op was transformed against ops at versions 6 and 7. The result is the transformed op. So we need to broadcast the transformed op, not the original.\n\nBut other clients (B, C) might have version 8 or 9 already? No, if they're connected to the same document, they should be roughly in sync. The server always applies ops in sequence, so:\n\n1. Server receives op from client A with version V\n2. Server transforms op against [V+1, V+N] → op'\n3. Server applies op' and increments to V+N+1\n4. Server broadcasts op' to all clients except A\n5. Server sends ack(op') to A\n\nOther clients receive op' and apply it to their local state. Since they're at version V+N (the server's version before applying), applying op' brings them to V+N+1 ✓\n\nHmm wait, but other clients might not know about the original op from client A. They just need to know what operation was actually applied (after transformation). So we broadcast the transformed operation.\n\nActually, another approach: just broadcast the new content and version. This is simpler but less efficient for bandwidth. However, for a coding challenge, it's fine.\n\nNo wait, if we broadcast the full content, then clients don't need to do any OT at all. They just replace their local state with the broadcast. That's much simpler and also correct.\n\nBut the challenge says \"send edits as operations\" and \"use OT to ensure convergence\". So I should send operations.\n\nLet me do this:\n1. Receive op from client (with version)\n2. Transform against intervening ops\n3. Apply and store\n4. Broadcast the TRANSFORMED operation and new version to all other clients\n5. They apply it directly (no transformation needed since it's already at their version)\n\nBut actually, what if a client has a pending local operation that hasn't been sent yet? Then they need to transform that pending op against the broadcast op. This is getting into client-side territory.\n\nFor this challenge, let me simplify: the server is the source of truth. When a client receives a broadcast, their application of the broadcast op is straightforward because the broadcast op is at the current version.\n\nBut I also need to handle the case where a client has sent an operation AND received a broadcast from another client, both at the same version. In that case:\n- Client sends op at version 5\n- Server receives op from another client, transforms it (was already at version 5), applies it, and broadcasts the transformed op at version 6\n- Our original client receives the broadcast at version 6\n- But our client's local op hasn't been acknowledged yet\n\nFor simplicity, I'll have the server broadcast the new content as part of the operation message, so clients just set the document content directly. But I'll also include the operation for OT-advanced clients.\n\nActually, the simplest approach for this challenge:\n\n**Server-side state per document:**\n- `content: string` - latest document content\n- `version: number` - current version number\n- `ops: Operation[]` - list of applied operations indexed by version\n\n**Client connects:**\n- Server sends: `{ type: \"sync\", content, version, docId }`\n\n**Client sends operation:**\n- Server receives: `{ type: \"operation\", operation, version }`\n- Server validates the operation\n- If version < currentVersion, transform operation against ops[version..currentVersion-1]\n- Apply the (possibly transformed) operation\n- Increment version\n- Store in DB\n- Broadcast to all OTHER clients: `{ type: \"operation\", operation: transformedOp, version: newVersion }`\n- Send ack to sender: `{ type: \"ack\", version: newVersion }`\n\nWait, I also need to think about what other clients do with the broadcast. They receive `{ type: \"operation\", operation, version }`. They should apply this to their local state. If they're at version newVersion - 1, they apply it directly. If not, they might need to transform.\n\nFor the challenge, I'll keep it simple:\n- When a client receives an operation broadcast, it can apply it (client just replaces content with what the server says)\n- Or the server can send the full content along with the operation\n\nLet me just include the content in the broadcast. That's the simplest correct approach:\n\nBroadcast: `{ type: \"edit\", operation: transformedOp, content: newContent, version: newVersion }`\n\nClients just set their content to the broadcast content. Simple and correct.\n\nActually, for the challenge, let me implement it properly with OT. The server broadcasts operations, and for simplicity, also includes the final content. Clients use the content to update their local state.\n\nOK let me implement this now.","signature":"16846457-3567-44ef-aee2-68f4ccb7be36"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":147,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":42880,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"782310fc-3819-4b54-9851-be0afa47ae6c","uuid":"a1991889-7b40-49b1-99b3-3b0ef1747704"}
