ients, they DO see the same order.\n\nSo the OT property I actually need is NOT:\napply(apply(s, A), B') = apply(apply(s, B), A')\n\nIt's: when the server processes operations sequentially, clients converge.\n\nThe server:\n1. Receives opA and opB (both at version V)\n2. Processes opA first (arrival order): applies opA, increments to V+1\n3. When processing opB: transforms opB against opA to get opB'\n4. Applies opB' at version V+1\n\nThe transform is: what does opB look like after opA?\n\nThis is a ONE-WAY transform. I don't need opA' (what A looks like after B). I only need opB' (what B looks like after A).\n\nSo the transform function can be one-way: `transformAgainst(op, priorOps)` where we transform op against priorOps sequentially.\n\nThe issue was in my test: I was testing the two-way transform (both A' and B'). But I only need one-way!\n\nSo for the test \"insert inside deleted range\":\n- If the server processes delete first: \"hello\" → \"ho\", then A's insert(2, \"X\") processed → A is at version 2 in original \"hello\", server is at version 2. Version(2) == room.version(2)? Or do I need to transform?\n\nWait, let me re-read my server code:\n\n```javascript\nif (version < room.version) {\n  const offset = Math.max(0, version - room.baseVersion);\n  const intervening = room.ops.slice(offset);\n  op = transformAgainst(op, intervening);\n}\n```\n\nIf A's version = 1 (from sync) and B's delete was processed first (advancing to version 2):\n- version(1) < room.version(2) → transform\n- intervening = room.ops.slice(1 - 1) = room.ops.slice(0) = [deleteOp]\n- transformAgainst(A, [deleteOp]) → what does A look like after the delete?\n\nThis should give insert(1, \"X\") (after B deletes, A inserts at position 1).\n\nThen after A is applied at version 2: \"ho\" → insert(1, \"X\") = \"hXo\".\n\nBoth clients see \"hXo\" at version 3. ✓\n\nNow what if A arrives first:\n- version(1) == room.version(1) → no transform, apply A → \"heXllo\" version 2.\n- B arrives at versi