+{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"90b3a20f-dd31-408d-a015-65f0198c9eb0","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good progress! All 22 OT unit tests pass. The integration tests have 3 failures:\n\n1. **Two-client convergence test timed out**: The websocket messages aren't being read properly. The test expects 2 messages but might only get 1. Need to fix timing/reading.\n\n2. **Three-client convergence test**: Expected length 4, got length 6. This means all 3 insert operations were applied (since initial \"... \" + 3 letters = 6). My OT is working correctly! The test expectation was wrong - I wrote \"expect(content.length).toBe(4)\" but the correct OT result has all 3 inserts applied. Let me fix the assertion.\n\n3. **Wrong-version test**: Got 'ack' instead of 'error'. The delete at pos 0, length 10 on content \"test\" (length 4) should throw \"out of range\" because 0+10 > 4. But it returned 'ack'. Let me check...\n\nWait, the client sent the delete without having received the first ack yet. The client sent:\n1. Insert at 0, text \"X\", version 99\n   - Server: version(99) >= room.version(2) → no transform\n   - Applies insert(0, \"X\") to \"test\" → \"Xtest\", version 3\n   - Sends ack\n\n2. Delete at 0, length 10, version 99\n   - Server: version(99) >= room.version(3) → no transform\n   - Applies delete(0, 10) to \"Xtest\" (length 5) → 0+10 > 5 → should throw!\n\nHmm, but it got ack. Let me re-check.\n\nActually, the client sends the insert FIRST at version 99. But there's a race condition: the insert and delete might be processed differently depending on message order and WebSocket timing. \n\nLooking at the test code more carefully:\n```javascript\nwsSend(ws, {\n  type: 'operation',\n  operation: { type: 'insert', pos: 0, text: 'X' },\n  version: 99,\n});\n\n// Meanwhile the delete is sent separately\nwsSend(ws, {\n  type: 'operation',\n  operation: { type: 'delete', pos: 0, length: 10 },\n  version: 99,\n});\n```\n\nBoth are sent with version 99. The first one (insert) succeeds because 99 >= room.version and the insert is valid. The second one (delete) also has version 99, but after the insert, room.version incremented to 3. So 99 >= 3 → no transform.\n\nBUT the delete(0, 10) on \"Xtest\" (length 5) should fail: 0+10=10 > 5. Let me check if applyOp throws...\n\nActually, in the test:\n```javascript\nwsSend(ws, {\n  type: 'operation',\n  operation: { type: 'insert', pos: 0, text: 'X' },\n  version: 99,\n});\nconst msg1 = await wsMessage(ws); // Should be ack or error\n\nwsSend(ws, {\n  type: 'operation',\n  operation: { type: 'delete', pos: 0, length: 10 },\n  version: 99,\n});\nconst errMsg = await wsMessage(ws); // Should be error\nexpect(errMsg.type).toBe('error');\n```\n\nWait, the first wsMessage should be the sync message (from connection), not the response to the insert. Let me look at the full test:\n\n```javascript\nit('rejects operation at wrong version', async () => {\n  const res = await request(handle.app)\n    .post('/api/documents')\n    .send({ title: 'Version Test', content: 'test' });\n  const docId = res.body.document.id;\n\n  const ws = new WebSocket(`ws://localhost:${port}/ws/${docId}`);\n  await wsMessage(ws);  // sync\n\n  // Send an operation with a future version number\n  wsSend(ws, {\n    type: 'operation',\n    operation: { type: 'insert', pos: 0, text: 'X' },\n    version: 99,\n  });\n\n  // OT transformation might fail or succeed depending on the case\n  // Actually 99 > room.version(1), so no transformation. Insert at 0 on \"test\" → \"Xtest\"\n  // This is actually OK behavior\n\n  // Let me test a stale version instead\n  wsSend(ws, {\n    type: 'operation',\n    operation: { type: 'delete', pos: 0, length: 10 },\n    version: 99,\n  });\n\n  const errMsg = await wsMessage(ws);\n  // Should error because delete out of range\n  expect(errMsg.type).toBe('error');\n\n  ws.close();\n});\n```\n\nThe issue: after the insert at version 99 (which succeeds), an ack is sent. Then the delete at version 99 is sent. But the error message for the delete might come after the ack for the insert. Or rather:\n\n- Message 1 (from insert): ack\n- Message 2 (from delete): error\n\nBut the code does:\n```\nconst msg1 = await wsMessage(ws); // This reads the sync... wait, it already did await wsMessage(ws) for sync.\n```\n\nLooking more carefully: the first `wsMessage` reads the sync. Then the second `wsMessage` reads... wait, there's NO message reading after the insert! The code is:\n\n```javascript\nawait wsMessage(ws);  // sync\n\nwsSend(ws, {\n  type: 'operation',\n  operation: { type: 'insert', pos: 0, text: 'X' },\n  version: 99,\n});\n\nwsSend(ws, {\n  type: 'operation',\n  operation: { type: 'delete', pos: 0, length: 10 },\n  version: 99,\n});\n\nconst errMsg = await wsMessage(ws);  // reads first available message\n```\n\nThe first available message after sending both operations could be:\n- The ack from the insert operation (server processes the insert first)\n- The error from the delete operation\n\nIf the ack comes first, `errMsg.type === 'ack'`. The test fails because it expects 'error'.\n\nThe race is: does the server send the ack before processing the delete? Since messages are processed sequentially in Node.js, the server will:\n1. Receive insert op → process → send ack\n2. Receive delete op → process → send error\n\nSo the first message read after both sends will be the ack.\n\nFix: either read the ack first, or test the version error differently (e.g., make the delete operation the only one sent).\n\nLet me fix these tests. I'll rewrite the failing tests to be more reliable.\n\nFor the two-client convergence test (timeout): The issue is with message reading. Let me simplify: use a timeout-based approach and check via REST.\n\nFor the three-client test (length 6): Fix the assertion to expect length 6.\nFor the wrong-version test: Read the ack first before sending the error-causing operation.\n\nAlso, I need to fix the \"database connection is not open\" error. This happens when the server's `stop()` is called while there are still WebSocket connections trying to clean up. The issue is in the `stop()` function: it closes the WebSocket first but the ws.close event handlers fire after the database is closed.\n\nThe fix: in `stop()`, close WebSockets, wait for them to close, then close the database.\n\nLet me fix these issues.","signature":"90b3a20f-dd31-408d-a015-65f0198c9eb0"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2142,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":108544,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"782310fc-3819-4b54-9851-be0afa47ae6c","uuid":"0b3cb12a-7437-4130-aa54-cfbdc5dd8475"}
